# Volume 01 — Ghar (Home)
# rule|consequence|story_core|cast|place|breach_reason|twist|final_image|family|tone
# Doors
Never answer the door after midnight|The voice outside learns yours|A young man home alone hears his sister begging to be let in; she is still at the wedding, and the voice is already inside.|YOUNG MAN; SISTER; THE VOICE|A second-floor flat above a tailor's shop|He thought he recognized his sister's voice|His sister is still at the wedding; the voice is already inside|The open door, the empty corridor, the phone glowing on the floor|threshold|eerie
Don't open the door if the bell rings twice|The second ring is an invitation|A mother expecting her son opens the door on the second ring; the corridor is empty, but a wet pair of shoes is already inside.|MOTHER; SON; NEIGHBOR|A ground-floor flat with a broken bell|Her son always rang twice — one long, one short — and the second ring matched his signal|The second ring came from inside the house|The wet shoes by the door, the chain still on|threshold|eerie
Never open the door with the chain still on|What enters is only as wide as the gap|A woman opens the door with the chain on to speak to a delivery boy; he asks her to sign, and a hand reaches through the gap.|WOMAN; DELIVERY BOY; THE HAND|A flat in a narrow lane|The parcel had her late husband's name on it, and she opened the gap to sign|The hand belongs to no one at the door|The chain swinging, the clipboard on the floor|threshold|eerie
Don't look through the peephole after sunset|It looks back|A woman alone hears a knock and checks the peephole; an eye fills it. She steps back. The eye is now on her side of the door.|WOMAN; THE EYE; NEIGHBOR|A third-floor flat with a fish-eye lens|She wanted to know who was there|The eye is on the inside of the peephole|The peephole dark, her own eye reflected|witness|eerie
Never leave the door ajar for the cat|Something else learns the gap|A girl leaves the door ajar for her cat; a stray follows it in, and by morning the cat won't enter the kitchen.|GIRL; MOTHER; THE STRAY|A ground-floor house with a cat flap|She didn't want the cat to wake the house|The stray has been sleeping in her bed, not the cat|The cat at the kitchen door, refusing to go in|contamination|eerie
Don't paint your front door black|The house stops recognizing you|A man paints the front door black to surprise his wife; that night his key won't turn, and a stranger answers the bell.|MAN; WIFE; STRANGER|A small house in a back lane|He wanted to surprise his wife|The stranger says he has lived there for years|The black door, the key still in the lock|threshold|unsettling
Never bolt a door with someone inside|You lock them in with it|A brother bolts the door from outside to prank his sister; when he opens it, she is standing in the corridor, asking why he locked her in from the inside.|BROTHER; SISTER; THE HOUSE|A two-room flat with a bolt outside|He wanted to scare her|The bolt was never touched from outside|The sister's wet footprints leading to the door|threshold|eerie
Don't answer a knock in your own voice|It takes the voice home|A man hears his own voice outside asking to be let in; he answers without thinking, and for the rest of the night his voice comes from the corridor.|MAN; WIFE; THE VOICE|A flat at the end of a corridor|He answered before he was awake|His voice is now outside, asking to be let in|His wife on the phone, hearing him speak from the hall|mimicry|eerie
Never let a guest answer the door for another guest|The house counts the wrong host|At a dinner party, a guest answers the door; the host doesn't recognize the new arrival, but every other guest does.|HOST; GUEST; NEW ARRIVAL|A dining room with a door to the corridor|The host was in the kitchen|The new arrival has been at the table all evening|The empty chair at the head of the table|invitation|unsettling
Don't keep the spare key under the mat|The house learns who else knows|A woman keeps a spare key under the mat; nothing is stolen, but every morning a chair has been moved closer to her bed.|WOMAN; LANDLORD; THE VISITOR|A ground-floor flat with a mat|She was tired of locking herself out|The chair is moved from the inside|The mat, the key, the chair by the bed|threshold|eerie
# Mirrors
Never look into a mirror with the lights off|Your reflection stays lit|A girl wakes at 3 AM and sees her face in the dark mirror, lit as if by a lamp; when she turns on the light, the reflection is a second late.|GIRL; MOTHER; THE REFLECTION|A bedroom with a dressing mirror|She heard a noise and went to look|The reflection is lit from a room that isn't there|Her face in the dark mirror, eyes open|reflection|eerie
Don't cover a mirror with a bedsheet|It learns the shape of your sleep|A widow covers the bedroom mirror after her husband's death; each morning the sheet is shaped like a sleeping body.|WIDOW; DAUGHTER; THE SHAPE|A bedroom with a full-length mirror|She couldn't bear to see herself|The shape under the sheet breathes|The sheet rising once, then still|reflection|sad
Never say a dead person's name into a mirror|They answer with your face|A son says his mother's name into the bathroom mirror on her birthday; the reflection answers in her voice, using his mouth.|SON; MOTHER'S VOICE; WIFE|A bathroom with a cabinet mirror|He missed her|The reflection speaks with his mouth but her voice|His face, her voice, the cabinet door open|mimicry|sad
Don't buy a mirror from a house where someone died|It remembers the last face|A couple buys an antique mirror from an estate sale; the wife keeps seeing a woman in it who looks like the previous owner.|HUSBAND; WIFE; THE WOMAN|A drawing room with a heavy mirror|It was cheap and beautiful|The woman in the mirror is wearing the wife's clothes|The mirror covered with a dupatta, the woman's outline|reflection|eerie
Never turn a mirror to face the wall|It turns back|A man turns a mirror to the wall after a bad dream; he wakes to find it facing him, and his reflection is still asleep.|MAN; WIFE; THE REFLECTION|A bedroom with a freestanding mirror|He didn't want to see it|The reflection is in bed while he is standing|The mirror facing the bed, the reflection's eyes closed|reflection|eerie
Don't let two mirrors face each other|Something walks between them|A salon owner places two mirrors opposite each other; a customer appears in both, but not in the chair between them.|SALON OWNER; CUSTOMER; THE FIGURE|A small salon with two mirrors|She wanted the room to look bigger|The customer in the mirrors is not in the room|The empty chair, two reflections sitting in it|reflection|unsettling
Never wipe a mirror with your bare hand|It keeps your fingerprints as a map|A servant wipes a mirror with her palm; the prints remain, and each morning they lead from the mirror to a different door.|SERVANT; MADAM; THE PRINTS|A haveli with a long corridor|She didn't have a cloth|The prints lead to a room no one uses|The mirror, the prints, the door at the end|witness|eerie
Don't hang a mirror opposite your bed|You watch yourself sleep; something watches with you|A newlywed bride hangs a mirror opposite the bed; she wakes to see three figures in it: her, her husband, and a woman between them.|BRIDE; GROOM; THE WOMAN|A wedding-room with a new mirror|She wanted to check her hair|The third figure is in the mirror only|The mirror at the foot of the bed, the bride alone|reflection|eerie
Never break a mirror and keep the pieces|Each piece keeps a different version of you|A man breaks a mirror and keeps the shards in a drawer; each shard shows him at a different age, and one shows him dead.|MAN; WIFE; THE SHARDS|A bathroom with a broken cabinet mirror|He didn't want to explain the break|One shard shows a room with no door|The drawer open, the shards glowing faintly|reflection|unsettling
Don't cover a mirror while someone sleeps|The room dreams for them|A mother covers the mirror while her son sleeps; he wakes speaking in an old woman's voice, describing a room he has never seen.|MOTHER; SON; THE OLD WOMAN|A child's bedroom with a small mirror|She thought the mirror was disturbing his sleep|The old woman is the previous owner of the house|The mirror uncovered, the son's reflection older|mimicry|eerie
# Stairs and rooftops
Never count the stairs after dark|The house adds one|A boy counts fourteen steps every night; one night he counts fifteen, and the extra step leads to a landing that isn't there.|BOY; MOTHER; THE LANDING|A narrow staircase in an old house|He was bored and couldn't sleep|The fifteenth step leads to a sealed room|The staircase, the extra step, the dark landing|counting|eerie
Don't look up the stairwell when someone calls your name|The voice learns your face|A girl hears her father call from upstairs; she looks up, and the voice says her name in a way her father never does.|GIRL; FATHER; THE VOICE|A stairwell with a skylight|She thought her father was home|Her father is at work; the voice is on the stairs|The skylight, a face pressed to the glass|mimicry|eerie
Never sleep on the roof alone|The sky remembers you|A boy sleeps on the roof on a hot night; he wakes with a second shadow that doesn't move when he does.|BOY; MOTHER; THE SHADOW|A rooftop with a charpai|The house was too hot|The shadow is cast by nothing|The charpai, two shadows, one boy|witness|eerie
Don't leave the rooftop door open at night|The city comes in|A family leaves the rooftop door open for air; in the morning there are wet footprints from the roof to the kitchen, and the city is silent.|FATHER; MOTHER; DAUGHTER|A rooftop door beside the kitchen|It was the hottest night of the year|The footprints start at the roof edge and end at the table|The open roof door, the empty street below|threshold|eerie
Never walk up the stairs backward|You arrive somewhere else|A drunk man walks upstairs backward for a bet; he arrives at his floor, but the corridor is mirrored and his flat number is wrong.|MAN; FRIEND; THE NEIGHBOR|A stairwell in a five-floor building|He wanted to win the bet|The floor is the same, but the building is not|The wrong flat number, the same furniture inside|threshold|unsettling
Don't keep a chair on the roof|Someone sits in it|A widow keeps her husband's chair on the roof; each evening the cushion is warm, and the view has changed.|WIDOW; SON; THE CHAIR|A rooftop with a city view|She couldn't throw his chair away|The chair faces a street that wasn't there before|The empty chair, the cushion dented|invitation|sad
Never whistle on the stairs|Something whistles back|A boy whistles on the stairs; a whistle answers from below, then from above, then from inside the wall.|BOY; SISTER; THE WHISTLE|A stairwell with a loose banister|He was calling the cat|The whistle is coming from inside the wall|The boy's ear against the plaster|mimicry|eerie
Don't let a guest go to the roof alone|They come back as two|A cousin visits and goes to the roof to smoke; he comes back with a friend no one invited, who eats nothing and leaves no shoes.|COUSIN; HOST; THE FRIEND|A rooftop with a low wall|He wanted a cigarette|The friend has been on the roof since before the cousin arrived|Two cups, one pair of shoes|invitation|unsettling
Never repair a broken stair at night|The house learns you can fix things|A handyman fixes a broken stair after midnight; the next morning a different stair is broken, and the tools are warm.|HANDYMAN; LANDLORD; THE HOUSE|An old wooden staircase|He wanted to finish the job|The house is breaking a stair for every one he fixes|The tools, the sawdust, the new broken step|routine|eerie
Don't stand at the top of the stairs and listen|The house talks about you|A woman stands at the top of the stairs to listen to the guests below; she hears her own voice telling them a secret she never told.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; GUESTS|A staircase overlooking a drawing room|She wanted to know what they thought of her|Her voice downstairs is telling the truth|The banister, the voices, the woman's hand over her mouth|witness|unsettling
# Kitchen
Never leave a knife pointing at the door|It invites the guest in|A cook leaves a knife on the counter pointing at the door; that night a guest arrives who nobody invited, and the knife is on the table.|COOK; MADAM; THE GUEST|A kitchen with a back door|She was called away mid-chop|The guest says the knife was left out for him|The knife on the table, the guest's empty plate|invitation|eerie
Don't cook for a guest who hasn't arrived|Someone else eats|A mother cooks her son's favorite meal before he arrives; the food is eaten, the plate is warm, and her son calls to say he isn't coming.|MOTHER; SON; THE EATER|A kitchen with a window over the street|She wanted to surprise him|The son is late because he is already at the house|The empty plate, the son's voice on the phone|invitation|sad
Never taste food that's still cooking for the dead|You join the meal|A neighbor tastes the food prepared for a funeral; that night she dreams of a table full of people who died in the neighborhood.|NEIGHBOR; WIDOW; THE GUESTS|A courtyard kitchen during a funeral|She was hungry and didn't think|The dead are eating the same food|The pot, the empty courtyard, the extra plates|contamination|eerie
Don't keep an empty pot on the stove overnight|It fills|A new bride leaves an empty pot on the stove; in the morning it is full of rice that no one cooked, and her mother-in-law won't eat it.|BRIDE; MOTHER-IN-LAW; THE POT|A kitchen with a clay stove|She was too tired to clean up|The rice is the same recipe as the previous bride's|The pot, the rice, the mother-in-law's silence|contamination|eerie
Never throw salt over your shoulder in someone else's kitchen|You feed what's behind you|A guest throws salt over her shoulder in her host's kitchen; the host finds salt trails leading to a locked room.|GUEST; HOST; THE ROOM|A kitchen with a locked pantry|She did it out of habit|The salt leads to a room that was sealed after a death|The salt trail, the pantry door, the host's face|contamination|eerie
Don't let the kettle whistle after midnight|Something answers the call|A man makes tea after midnight; the kettle whistles, and a second whistle comes from the hallway, exactly the same pitch.|MAN; WIFE; THE WHISTLE|A kitchen with a gas stove|He couldn't sleep|The second whistle is coming from inside the flat|The kettle, the steam, the hallway light|mimicry|eerie
Never leave half a cut lemon|The sourness stays|A woman cuts a lemon and leaves the other half on the counter; for a week everything she cooks tastes of metal, and her husband stops eating.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; THE LEMON|A small kitchen with a window|She only needed a slice|The other half is always fresh, never dries|The lemon half, the knife, the husband's full plate|contamination|eerie
Don't stir a pot counterclockwise|You undo someone's work|A daughter stirs her mother's curry counterclockwise; the curry tastes like a stranger's recipe, and her mother asks who taught her to cook.|DAUGHTER; MOTHER; THE CURRY|A kitchen with a steel pot|She was impatient|The recipe is from the woman who lived there before|The pot, the steam, the daughter's hands|contamination|sad
Never leave the fridge open at night|The cold learns your name|A boy leaves the fridge open; at 3 AM he hears his name from inside it, in his mother's voice.|BOY; MOTHER; THE FRIDGE|A kitchen with an old fridge|He was looking for leftovers|His mother is asleep; the voice is in the fridge|The fridge door open, the light, the boy's breath|mimicry|eerie
Don't eat the last roti|The house remembers who went hungry|A servant eats the last roti; that night the family hears a child crying in the kitchen, and the bread bin is full again.|SERVANT; MADAM; THE CHILD|A kitchen with a bread bin|He hadn't eaten since morning|The child is the previous servant's son|The bread bin, the last roti, the crying|debt|sad
# Bedroom and sleep
Never sleep with your feet facing the door|You invite the visitor in|A man sleeps with his feet toward the door; he wakes to find his feet on the floor and a pair of shoes beside the bed that aren't his.|MAN; WIFE; THE VISITOR|A bedroom with the door at the foot of the bed|He was too tired to change position|The shoes belong to a man who died in the room|The shoes, the open door, the man's bare feet|invitation|eerie
Don't keep a mirror at the foot of the bed|You sleep in two places|A couple keeps a mirror at the foot of the bed; the wife wakes to see herself still asleep in the reflection while she stands by the window.|HUSBAND; WIFE; THE REFLECTION|A bedroom with a dressing mirror|She liked the light it gave the room|The reflection is asleep; the wife is awake|The mirror, the sleeping reflection, the empty bed|reflection|eerie
Never say "come in" while half asleep|Something takes it as permission|A woman half-asleep says "come in" to a knock; she wakes to find the door open and a damp shape at the foot of the bed.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; THE SHAPE|A bedroom with a thin door|She thought it was her husband|Her husband is still at work; the shape is not him|The open door, the wet footprints, the bed|threshold|eerie
Don't sleep on a bed someone died in|The bed remembers|A tenant buys a secondhand bed; every morning the mattress has a body-shaped dent that isn't his, and the sheets smell of betel leaf.|TENANT; LANDLORD; THE DENT|A rented room with one bed|It was cheap and comfortable|The dent belongs to the previous tenant who died there|The dent, the betel leaf, the tenant on the floor|contamination|eerie
Never leave a glass of water by the bed|Something drinks|A man leaves a glass of water by his bed; in the morning the glass is empty, and there are lip marks on the rim that are too wide for his mouth.|MAN; WIFE; THE DRINKER|A bedroom with a nightstand|He was thirsty at night|The lip marks are from someone with no teeth|The glass, the marks, the man's dry mouth|contamination|eerie
Don't sleep with the wardrobe open|It watches you dress|A girl sleeps with the wardrobe open; every morning her clothes are arranged as if someone dressed her, and one dress is missing.|GIRL; MOTHER; THE WARDROBE|A bedroom with an old wooden wardrobe|She was too scared to close it in the dark|The missing dress is the one her grandmother was buried in|The open wardrobe, the empty hanger, the girl's hair|witness|eerie
Never wear a dead person's clothes to bed|You dream their last night|A widow wears her husband's shirt to bed; she dreams his last night in the hospital, but from his point of view, and wakes with his cough.|WIDOW; DAUGHTER; HUSBAND'S VOICE|A bedroom with a pile of clothes|She missed him|The dream includes the moment he died|The shirt, the empty bed, the widow's hand at her throat|contamination|sad
Don't keep a child's toy in an adult's bed|The child comes to claim it|A couple finds a child's doll in their bed; they put it on a shelf, and every morning it is back under the pillow.|HUSBAND; WIFE; THE DOLL|A bedroom with a shelf of old toys|It was left by a niece|The doll belongs to a child who died in the house|The doll under the pillow, the wife's face|invitation|eerie
Never sleep in a room with an unmarked door|The room counts you as a guest|A guest sleeps in a room with a small unmarked door; he wakes to find it open and a chair pulled up to the bed.|GUEST; HOST; THE CHAIR|A guest room with a narrow door|He was too polite to ask about it|The door leads to a room that was walled up|The open door, the chair, the guest's shoes|threshold|eerie
Don't make the bed immediately after someone leaves|The shape stays|A mother makes her son's bed right after he leaves for the city; the imprint of his body remains, and the pillow is warm every night.|MOTHER; SON; THE IMPRINT|A bedroom with a single bed|She wanted the room to look tidy|The imprint is warm because someone is sleeping in it|The made bed, the dent, the mother's hand|invitation|sad
# Bathroom and water
Never look into the bathroom mirror at 3 AM|It's not your reflection|A woman wakes at 3 AM and sees a man's face in the bathroom mirror; when she screams, her husband runs in and sees only her.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; THE MAN|A bathroom with a cabinet mirror|She heard the tap running|The man is the previous owner, and he is still there|The mirror, the tap, the husband's confused face|reflection|eerie
Don't leave the tap running overnight|Something follows the water|A man leaves the tap running; in the morning there are wet footprints from the bathroom to his bed, and the water bill is for a house twice the size.|MAN; WIFE; THE FOOTPRINTS|A bathroom with a leaking tap|He forgot to turn it off|The footprints are webbed|The tap, the water, the footprints on the tiles|contamination|eerie
Never use the last bucket of water|The house gives you someone else's|A servant uses the last bucket of water; the water smells of perfume, and a woman's hair is wrapped around the handle.|SERVANT; MADAM; THE HAIR|A bathroom with a concrete tub|There was no other water|The hair belongs to the previous maid who disappeared|The bucket, the hair, the servant's wet hands|contamination|eerie
Don't sing in the shower after midnight|Someone harmonizes|A girl sings in the shower after midnight; a second voice joins in, perfectly, from the other side of the curtain.|GIRL; MOTHER; THE VOICE|A bathroom with a plastic curtain|She thought she was alone|The voice is her own, but older|The curtain, the steam, the girl's silence|mimicry|eerie
Never leave the bathroom door open while you sleep|The steam remembers|A couple sleeps with the bathroom door open; in the morning the mirror is fogged with a handprint from the inside.|HUSBAND; WIFE; THE HAND|A bedroom attached to a bathroom|It was hot and they wanted air|The handprint is smaller than either of theirs|The fogged mirror, the small handprint|witness|eerie
Don't use a towel that isn't yours|It dries someone else|A guest uses a towel in the bathroom; it smells of a stranger, and when he wipes his face, the towel is wet with someone else's tears.|GUEST; HOST; THE STRANGER|A guest bathroom with a towel rack|He didn't bring his own|The towel belongs to a previous guest who never left|The towel, the guest's face, the wet floor|contamination|unsettling
Never wash your face in the dark|You wash off your face|A boy washes his face in the dark; when he turns on the light, his face is slightly different, and his mother asks who he is.|BOY; MOTHER; THE FACE|A bathroom with a single bulb|He didn't want to wake anyone|The face is the boy from the mirror|The sink, the boy's new face, the mother's scream|reflection|eerie
Don't throw water on the floor to clean it|The floor drinks|A new maid throws water on the bathroom floor; the floor stays wet for days, and a stain grows in the shape of a woman.|MAID; MADAM; THE STAIN|A bathroom with cracked tiles|She was told to clean quickly|The stain is the previous maid, who drowned in the tub|The wet floor, the stain, the maid's mop|contamination|eerie
Never share a bath with a stranger|The water keeps a copy|A traveler shares a bath with a stranger at a hostel; the water is warm, and when he gets out, the stranger is still in the tub, wearing his face.|TRAVELER; STRANGER; THE HOSTEL KEEPER|A shared bathroom in a hostel|There was only one bath|The stranger is the traveler's reflection|The tub, the stranger, the traveler's face|reflection|unsettling
Don't leave the bathroom light on for a child|The child sees what you don't|A mother leaves the bathroom light on for her son; he says there is a woman in the mirror who wants to meet her, and describes her mother's dress.|MOTHER; SON; THE WOMAN|A bathroom with a night light|He was afraid of the dark|The woman is the mother's own mother, who died before the son was born|The night light, the mirror, the son's drawing|witness|sad
# Guests and empty chairs
Never keep an empty chair at dinner|Someone fills it|A family keeps an empty chair for a relative who died; one night a guest sits in it, eats nothing, and leaves a wet print on the seat.|FATHER; MOTHER; THE GUEST|A dining room with five chairs|They couldn't let go|The guest is the relative, but he doesn't know he's dead|The empty chair, the wet print, the family's silence|invitation|eerie
Don't set a place for a guest who didn't come|They come later|A mother sets a place for her son who cancels dinner; at midnight someone eats the food, and the plate is licked clean.|MOTHER; SON; THE EATER|A dining table set for two|She hoped he would change his mind|The son is asleep at his own flat; the eater is someone else|The empty plate, the son's photo, the mother's hand|invitation|sad
Never let a guest leave without water|They take the thirst with them|A host forgets to offer water to a guest; the guest leaves, and for a week the host's mouth is dry no matter how much he drinks.|HOST; GUEST; WIFE|A veranda with a water cooler|He was in a hurry|The guest was not thirsty; he was measuring the host's manners|The empty glass, the host's cracked lips|debt|eerie
Don't ask a guest their name after sunset|They give you yours|A host asks a stranger's name after sunset; the stranger says it, and the host forgets his own name for three days.|HOST; STRANGER; WIFE|A veranda at dusk|He wanted to be polite|The stranger's name is the host's childhood nickname|The stranger's smile, the host's ID card|mimicry|eerie
Never seat a guest facing the door|They watch who comes|A host seats a guest facing the door; the guest keeps greeting people who aren't there, and the host turns to see an empty corridor.|HOST; GUEST; THE VISITORS|A drawing room with a door behind the guest|It was the only comfortable chair|The guest is greeting the dead who used to live there|The guest's raised hand, the empty doorway|witness|eerie
Don't keep a guest's shoes by the door overnight|They walk back|A family leaves a guest's shoes by the door overnight; in the morning the shoes are muddy and the guest is still asleep, but his feet are clean.|HOST; GUEST; THE SHOES|A narrow entrance hall|They didn't want to wake him|The shoes have been walking all night|The muddy shoes, the sleeping guest, his clean feet|contamination|eerie
Never let a guest sleep in the master bedroom|The house prefers them|A family gives the master bedroom to a guest; the guest stays for a month, and the family starts sleeping in the hall.|HOST; GUEST; WIFE|A master bedroom with a large bed|The guest was family|The guest has been dead for years and the house is keeping him|The guest in the bed, the family on the floor|invitation|unsettling
Don't serve a guest food you didn't cook|They taste the cook's secret|A host serves a guest food cooked by someone else; the guest says it tastes like a woman's regret, and the cook won't come out of the kitchen.|HOST; GUEST; COOK|A dining room with a kitchen behind|The host couldn't cook|The cook is the guest's dead wife|The guest's plate, the kitchen door, the cook's shadow|contamination|sad
Never ask a guest to stay one more night|They take it literally|A host jokingly asks a guest to stay one more night; the guest stays for a year, and the host's family forgets he wasn't always there.|HOST; GUEST; DAUGHTER|A small house with one guest room|He didn't want to be rude|The guest has no home and no memory of arriving|The guest at the table, the family's new photo|invitation|unsettling
Don't clean the room immediately after a guest leaves|You clean them away|A maid cleans a guest room right after the guest leaves; she finds a photograph in the dust and the guest is in it, standing behind her.|MAID; MADAM; GUEST|A guest room with a dressing table|She wanted to finish early|The guest never left; the maid is cleaning around him|The photo, the dust, the maid's reflection|witness|eerie
# Photos and walls
Never hang a photo of someone who left|They come back for it|A wife hangs a photo of her husband who left; that night she hears the front door open and his footsteps stop in front of the photo.|WIFE; HUSBAND; THE PHOTO|A drawing room with a photo wall|She missed him|The husband is dead; the footsteps are someone else|The photo, the open door, the muddy boots|invitation|sad
Don't keep a photo with a stranger in the background|The stranger remembers|A family keeps a wedding photo with a man no one recognizes in the background; the man starts appearing in new photos, closer each time.|FATHER; MOTHER; THE STRANGER|A drawing room with a family album|They thought he was a distant cousin|The stranger is the photographer who died at the wedding|The album, the stranger's face, the family's empty chairs|witness|eerie
Never turn a photo face down|It turns back|A son turns his father's photo face down after an argument; every morning it is upright, and the glass is warm.|SON; MOTHER; THE PHOTO|A bedroom with a bedside photo|He was angry|His father is dead, and the photo is warm because he is holding it|The upright photo, the son's hand, the warm glass|invitation|sad
Don't hang a mirror opposite a family photo|The photo moves|A hallway has a mirror opposite a family photo; one morning the photo is a mirror image, and the youngest child is on the wrong side.|MOTHER; FATHER; THE CHILD|A hallway with a mirror and a photo|They liked the light|The child in the photo is not the child in the house|The photo, the mirror, the child's empty bed|reflection|eerie
Never take a photo of a sleeping person|You keep a piece of their sleep|A brother takes a photo of his sister sleeping; she wakes exhausted every day, and the photo shows her eyes open.|BROTHER; SISTER; MOTHER|A bedroom with a phone|He thought it was funny|The sister in the photo is awake and asking for help|The phone, the photo, the sister's closed eyes|witness|eerie
Don't carry a dead person's photo|They follow your money|A widow keeps her husband's photo in her wallet; every time she pays, the shopkeeper says her husband was just there.|WIDOW; SHOPKEEPER; HUSBAND|A shop counter with a cash box|She couldn't remove him|The husband is spending her money in a different shop|The wallet, the photo, the shopkeeper's smile|debt|sad
Never nail a photo to a wall|The wall remembers the face|A tenant nails a photo to the wall; when he removes it, the face is in the plaster, and it changes expression.|TENANT; LANDLORD; THE FACE|A rented room with bare walls|He wanted it to stay straight|The face is the previous tenant, who was nailed into the wall|The nail, the plaster, the face's open mouth|contamination|eerie
Don't hang a wedding photo where none happened|The house plans one|A bachelor displays a wedding photo he found; his family starts receiving wedding invitations addressed to him, and a bride arrives.|BACHELOR; MOTHER; THE BRIDE|A drawing room with a single photo|He liked the frame|The bride is the woman in the photo, and she is dead|The photo, the bride at the door, the bachelor's ring|invitation|unsettling
Never cut a person out of a photo|The missing piece looks for a body|A sister cuts her brother out of a family photo; he starts appearing in the background of every new photo, always at the edge.|SISTER; MOTHER; BROTHER|A kitchen table with scissors|She was angry at him|The brother is dead, and the photos are his way back|The cut photo, the brother's shadow, the sister's scissors|witness|sad
Don't keep an empty photo frame|It fills|A couple keeps an empty frame on the mantel; one morning there is a photo of them in it, taken from inside their bedroom.|HUSBAND; WIFE; THE PHOTO|A bedroom with a mantel|They were waiting for a good picture|The photo was taken while they slept|The frame, the photo, the camera on the shelf|witness|eerie
# Lights and switches
Never turn off the last light in the house|The dark counts the rooms|A man turns off the last light; in the dark he hears a door open in a room that doesn't exist, and the light switch is warm.|MAN; WIFE; THE ROOM|A small house with one hallway light|He wanted to save electricity|The extra room appears only in the dark|The switch, the dark hallway, the new door|threshold|eerie
Don't fix a flickering bulb alone|It flickers for a reason|A father fixes a flickering bulb alone; it stops flickering, but now the light stays on even when he unscrews it.|FATHER; DAUGHTER; THE BULB|A corridor with a single bulb|He was tired of the noise|The bulb is drawing power from the daughter's room|The bulb, the unscrewed socket, the daughter's dark room|routine|eerie
Never leave a light on in an empty room|Someone uses it|A mother leaves a light on in her son's empty room; one night she sees a shadow studying at his desk, and the chair is warm.|MOTHER; SON; THE SHADOW|A bedroom with a desk|She missed him|The shadow is the son, but he is asleep in another city|The desk, the shadow, the warm chair|witness|sad
Don't switch on a light you didn't install|You light someone else's room|A tenant switches on a light in a sealed room; the light reveals furniture from another house, and a family sitting at dinner.|TENANT; LANDLORD; THE FAMILY|A rented house with a sealed room|He was looking for the fuse box|The family is the previous owners, still eating|The light, the family, the tenant's hand on the switch|threshold|eerie
Never sleep with the hallway light off|The hallway moves|A girl sleeps with the hallway light off; she wakes to find the hallway longer, and a door at the end that wasn't there.|GIRL; MOTHER; THE HALLWAY|A narrow hallway with three doors|She was too old for a night light|The hallway is copying the house next door|The dark hallway, the new door, the girl's feet|threshold|eerie
Don't replace a dead bulb at night|The old one keeps burning|A man replaces a dead bulb at night; the old bulb, now in the bin, is still glowing, and it is getting brighter.|MAN; WIFE; THE BULB|A kitchen with a bin|He needed light|The old bulb is lighting a room in the basement|The bin, the glowing bulb, the basement stairs|routine|eerie
Never light a candle for someone who isn't dead|The flame waits|A daughter lights a candle for her father who is in the hospital; the flame burns all night, and in the morning her father says he saw it.|DAUGHTER; FATHER; THE FLAME|A bedside table with a candle|She was worried|The candle is for the father's roommate, who died that night|The candle, the hospital phone, the daughter's hand|debt|sad
Don't turn on all the lights at once|The house sees you clearly|A new homeowner turns on every light; the house is bright, and in every window a different room is reflected.|HOMEOWNER; WIFE; THE NEIGHBOR|A house with many windows|He wanted to check the wiring|The reflections show rooms from the previous house|The bright house, the windows, the neighbor's face|witness|eerie
Never leave the porch light on after a guest leaves|They think they're invited|A host leaves the porch light on after a guest leaves; the guest returns every night and stands under it, waiting to be let in.|HOST; GUEST; WIFE|A porch with a yellow bulb|He forgot to turn it off|The guest never left; he has been standing there for years|The porch light, the guest's silhouette, the host's door|invitation|eerie
Don't unscrew a bulb and leave the socket empty|Something plugs in|A boy unscrews a bulb and leaves the socket empty; at night he hears a faint hum from the socket, and a new light appears in the wall.|BOY; MOTHER; THE HUMMING|A bedroom with a bare socket|He wanted to fix his lamp|The socket is feeding a room behind the wall|The socket, the hum, the new light in the plaster|contamination|eerie
# Sounds at night
Never answer a knock that comes from inside|The house wants out|A man hears a knock from inside the cupboard; he opens it, and the knock is now coming from the front door.|MAN; WIFE; THE KNOCK|A bedroom with a built-in cupboard|He thought it was a rat|The house is knocking to be let out|The cupboard, the front door, the man's hand|threshold|eerie
Don't follow a sound that stops when you listen|It's learning your steps|A woman follows a sound in the hallway; every time she stops, it stops, and when she moves, it moves half a second later.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; THE SOUND|A long hallway with a carpet|She thought someone had broken in|The sound is copying her footsteps from inside the wall|The hallway, the carpet, the woman's bare feet|mimicry|eerie
Never whistle back at a whistle|It knows your tune|A boy whistles back at a whistle outside; the whistle answers with the tune his father used to whistle, and his father has been dead for years.|BOY; MOTHER; THE WHISTLE|A rooftop at dusk|He thought it was a neighbor|The whistle is coming from his father's grave|The rooftop, the boy's lips, the empty street|mimicry|sad
Don't ignore a child crying in an empty room|It learns you can hear|A couple ignores a child crying in the empty nursery; the crying stops, and the next night it comes from under their bed.|HUSBAND; WIFE; THE CHILD|A flat with an empty nursery|They thought it was the neighbors|The child is the one they lost, and it wants to be heard|The nursery, the empty crib, the bed|witness|sad
Never turn up the TV to drown out a noise|The noise gets louder|A man turns up the TV to drown out a scratching in the wall; the scratching matches the volume, and the TV starts showing the wall.|MAN; WIFE; THE SCRATCHING|A living room with a TV against the wall|He didn't want to hear it|The scratching is coming from inside the TV|The TV, the wall, the man's remote|mimicry|eerie
Don't sleep through a phone ringing at 3 AM|It calls everyone you know|A woman ignores a 3 AM call; the next night her mother calls at 3 AM, and the night after that, her brother.|WOMAN; MOTHER; THE CALLER|A bedside table with a phone|She thought it was a wrong number|The caller is using her contacts one by one|The phone, the call log, the woman's face|departure|eerie
Never mimic a sound you don't recognize|It learns your voice|A girl mimics a clicking sound from the wall; the clicking stops, and that night her mother hears her voice calling from the kitchen.|GIRL; MOTHER; THE CLICKING|A bedroom with a wall vent|She was playing|The voice in the kitchen is the girl's, but the girl is in bed|The vent, the kitchen, the mother's hand|mimicry|eerie
Don't open the window to hear better|The outside hears you|A man opens the window to hear a distant song; the song stops, and every window in the house is now open.|MAN; WIFE; THE SONG|A bedroom with a window to the street|He wanted to hear the singer|The singer is in the house, and the windows are his way out|The open windows, the empty street, the man's wife|threshold|eerie
Never laugh at a sound in an empty house|It laughs back|A bachelor laughs at a creak in the empty house; a second laugh comes from the kitchen, using his own laugh.|BACHELOR; FRIEND; THE LAUGH|A kitchen with an open door|He thought it was the house settling|The laugh is his, but he isn't laughing|The kitchen door, the bachelor's smile, the second laugh|mimicry|eerie
Don't say "it's nothing" out loud|It takes you at your word|A wife tells her husband a noise is nothing; the noise stops, and the next morning his side of the bed is empty and the window is open.|HUSBAND; WIFE; THE NOISE|A bedroom with a window|She didn't want to scare him|The noise was the husband leaving|The empty bed, the open window, the wife's hand|departure|sad
